          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Mr Krishnamurthy is on eight medications for multiple conditions, and his daughter asks which problem to treat first. What principle should guide the answer in geriatric medicine?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treat the condition with the most abnormal laboratory value first</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lab abnormalities don't tell you what's actually disabling the patient — geriatric care prioritises differently.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treat the problem most threatening his independence and quality of life</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the core reframing of geriatric medicine: function and independence, not the worst number on a report, determines priority.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treat the most recently diagnosed condition first</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recency of diagnosis has no bearing on which problem is most disabling — this isn't how priority is set in elderly care.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treat the condition prescribed by the most senior specialist first</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Specialist hierarchy is irrelevant to clinical priority; each specialist may be treating only one piece of a multimorbid picture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Not quite — review how the video reframes priority-setting in multimorbid elderly patients: it's about what threatens independence, not the worst lab value."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — geriatric medicine reframes priority around function and independence, not isolated disease severity."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
