          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Mr Nair's delirium was agitated and easy to spot. Which subtype of delirium is a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Mr Nair's delirium was agitated and easy to spot. Which subtype of delirium is actually the most common overall, yet the one most often missed by clinicians?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperactive delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyperactive delirium, like Mr Nair's, is agitated and easy to recognise — but it is not the most common subtype.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypoactive delirium</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypoactive delirium is quiet and withdrawn, often mistaken for depression — it is both the most common subtype and the one most frequently missed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mixed delirium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mixed delirium switches between hyperactive and hypoactive features but is not the most common subtype.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Delirium associated with alcohol withdrawal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Alcohol withdrawal delirium is a specific cause requiring benzodiazepines, not a general subtype classification.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the three delirium subtypes — hypoactive delirium is the most common yet most frequently missed because it looks like depression."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — hypoactive delirium is the quiet, commonly missed subtype; always screen with CAM even in withdrawn patients."
                }
              ],
