          "label": "<p>Before labelling an elderly patient's cognitive decline as dementia, which set o</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Before labelling an elderly patient's cognitive decline as dementia, which set of reversible causes must always be excluded first?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The three Ds and three Ts — depression, delirium, drugs; thyroid problems, thiamine deficiency, toxins</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These six reversible causes can mimic or coexist with dementia, and must be ruled out before the diagnosis is made.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>PINCH ME — pain, infection, nutrition/constipation, hypoxia, medication, environment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PINCH ME is the mnemonic for delirium precipitants, not for ruling out reversible causes of apparent dementia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The four malnutrition mechanisms — eat less, absorb less, metabolic shift, increased demand</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This mnemonic explains why malnutrition develops in older adults; it is unrelated to dementia work-up.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Beers Criteria and STOPP/START</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are tools for reviewing inappropriate or missing medications, not for excluding reversible causes of cognitive decline.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the reversible mimics of dementia: the three Ds (depression, delirium, drugs) and three Ts (thyroid, thiamine, toxins)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — always rule out the 3 Ds and 3 Ts before diagnosing dementia, since delirium superimposed on dementia is the most common presentation in hospitalised elderly patients."
                }
              ],
